Build eval/run_agent_eval.py: a local agent-eval runner per docs/EVAL_RUNNER_SPEC.md (read it, and eval/kaggle/gold_calibration.py, first).
Harness is the installed swegemma 0.2.11 in /home/ubuntu/gemma-local/venv (python: /home/ubuntu/gemma-local/venv/bin/python, built on the image-mirror python /home/ubuntu/gemma-local/pyimg). Read its source (swegemma/cli.py `eval` subcommand, evaluate.py Evaluator, harness/agent_runner.py, harness/verification.py, harness/sample_verification.py, harness/container_setup.py, sandbox/subprocess.py) to pick the integration point; prefer driving Evaluator/EvalConfig programmatically (same as cli.py does) over shelling out, so the patches apply in-process.

CLI: python eval/run_agent_eval.py --bundle <bundle dir with agent.yaml> --data-dir data/raw --split eval/splits/dev.txt [--ids id1,id2] --run-name NAME --concurrency N [--max-tool-calls K --max-turns T --session-minutes M --command-timeout S] [--endpoint URL (sets LOCAL_INFERENCE_URL/OPENAI_BASE_URL)] [--models-yaml path] [--skip-selfcheck] [--selfcheck-ids a,b]
Requirements:
1. Env guard: fail fast unless sys._base_executable / sys.base_prefix resolves under /home/ubuntu/gemma-local/pyimg (overridable --allow-foreign-python for unit tests only). Sandbox must be 'subprocess'.
2. Dependency patch: import and reuse patch_subprocess_deps from eval/kaggle/gold_calibration.py (do not copy its logic). Extend it (small change in gold_calibration.py is fine, keep its tests passing) so the patched install function is bound in EVERY module that holds a name binding: swegemma.harness.container_setup, swegemma.harness.verification, swegemma.harness.sample_verification, swegemma.evaluate, plus any other module in the swegemma package found by scanning sys.modules after importing swegemma.evaluate/cli/agent_runner for an attribute `install_test_dependencies` equal to the original. Then assert all such bindings `is` the patched function before the first task; abort otherwise.
3. Self-check before the agent run (unless --skip-selfcheck): run the gold patch for 2 tasks (default: first 2 ids of the split in sorted order that are in eval/splits/clean.txt) through the same verification path gold_calibration uses (reuse its functions); abort non-zero if either is not resolved. Gold patches must never reach the agent.
4. Run the agent on split ids (∩ --ids if given) with concurrency N against one endpoint. Resumable: results/<run-name>.jsonl; skip ids already present; append each finished task atomically (one line, flush+fsync).
5. Results file: first line is a schema header JSON {"_schema": "agent_eval_v1", "fields": {...descriptions...}, "bundle": path, "bundle_sha256": sha of agent.yaml+tree, "git_sha": repo HEAD, "split": path + sha256, "caps": {...}, "concurrency": N, "endpoint": URL, "created_utc": ...}. If the file exists with a different header (bundle/caps/split), refuse unless --force-new. Per-task row: instance_id, repo, resolved (bool), exit_code, error, wall_seconds (agent phase and verification separately, plus total), llm_turns, tool_calls (total and per tool name), prompt_tokens, completion_tokens (from the trace if the harness records them; else null — do not guess), patch_bytes, patch_files (list), test_files_edited (paths in the agent patch matching test patterns, the same rule the harness uses to strip test edits if it has one, else tests?/ or test_*.py or *_test.py or conftest.py), stray_files (new files in the patch that are not .py source under the package, e.g. scratch scripts in /workspace), empty_patch (bool), timed_out (bool), trace_path.
6. Raw per-task traces/outputs go to runs/<run-name>/ (gitignored). Never write task text or patches into results/.
7. Stub endpoint for tests: eval/stub_openai.py, a tiny stdlib-only OpenAI-compatible /v1/chat/completions server (and /v1/models) that plays a scripted sequence of responses from a JSON file (e.g. a tool call then a final message), so the runner can be exercised end to end offline without a GPU. Support tool_calls in the response format litellm expects.
8. Tests in eval/tests/test_run_agent_eval.py (pytest, offline, fast): binding-patch assertion catches an unpatched module; results header/resume logic (skip finished, refuse mismatched header); test_files_edited/stray_files classification on synthetic diffs; schema header written once. Plus one opt-in integration test (skip unless env RUN_AGENT_EVAL_E2E=1) that starts the stub, runs bundles/e4b/v000 on 1 clean dev task at concurrency 1 with --skip-selfcheck, and asserts a row is written. Run the unit tests with /home/ubuntu/gemma-local/venv/bin/python -m pytest eval/tests -q and report output. If the sandbox allows, also run the E2E test once and report.
Constraints: no new third-party deps beyond what the venv has; keep RAM low; no network except localhost; do not touch data/, bundles/, paper/, ops/ (other than your report). Keep the code compact and readable (target < 450 lines for the runner).
